#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:2
msgid "|morpheus| Blueprints"
msgstr "|morpheus| Blueprints"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:4
msgid ""
"|morpheus| App Blueprints allow pre-configured full multi-tier "
"application deployments for multiple environments. Blueprints can be "
"provisioned from the |ProApp| section and can be fully configured for one"
" click provisioning. Blueprints can be built within the ``Builder`` "
"section or by code in the ``Raw`` section. Blueprints can also be "
"exported as YAML or JSON and created with the |morpheus| API and CLI."
msgstr "|morpheus| App Blueprints allow pre-configured full multi-tier application deployments for multiple environments. Blueprints can be provisioned from the |ProApp| section and can be fully configured for one click provisioning. Blueprints puede ser built within the ``Builder`` section or by code in the ``Raw`` section. Blueprints can also be exported as YAML or JSON and created with the |morpheus| API and CLI."

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:6
msgid ""
"A unique capability of the YAML/JSON based Morpheus blueprint structure "
"is the ability to have multiple configurations per instance being "
"provisioned within the app blueprint. This can be a scoped configuration "
"that acts as overrides based on selected cloud, group, and/or environment"
" the app is being provisioned in as a target. For example. maybe the "
"\"development\" environment doesn't need as many horizontally scaled "
"nodes as the \"production\" environment. Another great aspect of this "
"configuration markup is a blueprint can be defined as a hybrid cloud "
"blueprint. This makes the app blueprint structure very powerful and in "
"some ways better than alternative infrastructure as code orchestrators. "
"For Example, ARM is locked into Azure, while CloudFormation is locked "
"into AWS. Even Terraform does not allow a tf file to expand its bounds "
"beyond a specific provider type."
msgstr "A unique capability of the YAML/JSON based Morpheus blueprint structure is the ability to have multiple configurations per instance being provisioned within the app blueprint. This can be a scoped configuration that acts as overrides based on selected cloud, group, and/or environment the app is being provisioned in as a target. For example. maybe the \"development\" environment doesn't need as many horizontally scaled nodes as the \"production\" environment. Another great aspect of this configuration markup is a blueprint puede ser defined as a hybrid cloud blueprint. This makes the app blueprint structure very powerful and in some ways better than alternative infrastructure as code orchestrators. For Example, ARM is locked into Azure, while NubeFormation is locked into AWS. Even Terraform does not allow a tf file to expand its bounds beyond a specific provider type."

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:9
msgid "Basic Blueprint Structure"
msgstr "Basic Blueprint Structure"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:11
msgid ""
"In a Morpheus App Blueprint there are a few structural concepts to be "
"aware of. Firstly there is a concept of a `Tier`. A `Tier` is a grouping "
"of instances within an app blueprint. Tiers can be used for a variety of "
"things including sequenced booting of instances or even properly creating"
" endpoint groups and security group contexts in network security tools "
"like Cisco ACI. An example of a Tier structure might be a ``Web`` tier "
"and a ``Database`` tier. These tiers can also be marked as connected such"
" that network communication rules can appropriately be defined. A basic 2"
" Tier blueprint skeleton might look something like this:"
msgstr "In a Morpheus App Blueprint there are a few structural concepts to be aware of. Firstly there is a concept of a `Tier`. A `Tier` is a grouping of instances within an app blueprint. Tiers puede ser used for a variety of things including sequenced booting of instances or even properly creating endpoint groups and security group contexts in network security tools like Cisco ACI. An example of a Tier structure might be a ``Web`` tier and a ``Database`` tier. These tiers can also be marked as connected such that network communication rules can appropriately be defined. A basic 2 Tier blueprint skeleton might look something like this:"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:13
msgid ""
"name: Tier Example\n"
"type: morpheus\n"
"tiers:\n"
"  Web:\n"
"    linkedTiers:\n"
"      - Database\n"
"    tier:\n"
"      bootOrder: 1\n"
"    instances:\n"
"  Database:\n"
"    tier:\n"
"      bootOrder: 0\n"
"    instances:"
msgstr "name: Tier Example\ntype: morpheus\ntiers:\n  Web:\n    linkedTiers:\n      - Database\n    tier:\n      bootOrder: 1\n    instances:\n  Database:\n    tier:\n      bootOrder: 0\n    instances:"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:30
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"This example has defined 2 tiers as yaml properties under the `tiers` "
"object. They are called `Web` and `Database`. A Tier can optionally "
"define its connected tiers which are bi-directional even though only one "
"tier has to define them. This is the `linkedTiers` array and simply lists"
" the connected tiers by tier name. A Boot Order can also optionally be "
"defined under a nested ``{\"tier\": {\"bootOrder\": 1}}`` object "
"structure."
msgstr "Este/Esta example tiene definido/definida 2 tiers como yaml properties under el/la `tiers` object. They son called `Web` y `Database`. Un/Una Tier puede/n optionally define its connected tiers el/la cual son bi-directional even though solo one tier tiene a define them. Este/Esta es el/la `linkedTiers` array y simply lists el/la connected tiers por tier name. Un/Una Boot Order puede/n también optionally be definido/definida under un/una nested ``{\"tier\": {\"bootOrder\": 1}}`` object structure."

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:33
msgid "Configuration Scopes"
msgstr "Configuration Scopes"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:35
msgid ""
"Another capability of Morpheus App Blueprint structure is its "
"configuration scoping. This allows properties to be overridden based on "
"the apps target environment or even target group and cloud. For example. "
"Maybe we want to use a larger plan size in production vs. development"
msgstr "Another capability of Morpheus App Blueprint structure is its configuration scoping. This permite a properties to be overridden based on the apps target environment or even target group and cloud. For example. Maybe we want use a larger plan size in production vs. development"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:37
msgid "An example of that can be done using \"environments\" overrides."
msgstr "An example of that puede ser done using \"environments\" overrides."

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:39
msgid ""
"name: Simple Nginx\n"
"type: morpheus\n"
"tiers:\n"
"  Web:\n"
"    instances:\n"
"      - instance:\n"
"          type: docker\n"
"          name: Sample Nginx\n"
"        clouds:\n"
"          AWS Cali:\n"
"            instance:\n"
"              layout:\n"
"                code: docker-1.7-single\n"
"            config:\n"
"              dockerImageVersion: latest\n"
"              dockerRegistryId: ''\n"
"              dockerImage: nginx\n"
"            plan:\n"
"              code: container-128\n"
"         environments:\n"
"           Production:\n"
"             groups:\n"
"               All Clouds Demo:\n"
"                 clouds:\n"
"                   AWS Cali:\n"
"                     plan:\n"
"                       code: container-256"
msgstr "name: Simple Nginx\ntype: morpheus\ntiers:\n  Web:\n    instances:\n      - instance:\n          type: docker\n          name: Sample Nginx\n        clouds:\n          AWS Cali:\n            instance:\n              layout:\n                code: docker-1.7-single\n            config:\n              dockerImageVersion: latest\n              dockerRegistryId: ''\n              dockerImage: nginx\n            plan:\n              code: container-128\n         environments:\n           Production:\n             groups:\n               All Nubes Demo:\n                 clouds:\n                   AWS Cali:\n                     plan:\n                       code: container-256"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:70
msgid ""
"Note the new environments object. The object graph of the morpheus "
"blueprint structure gets merged and flattened at provision time based on "
"the scope of the configurations provided as well as the users target "
"cloud, group, and environment selection. In the Above example, a "
"selective override was done for the `AWS Cali` cloud when using a "
"Production Environment and deploying to the group `All Clouds Demo`. This"
" specific example changes the plan to a larger size. Scoped "
"configurations have various levels of precedence. Cloud is the lowest "
"level of precedence. a cloud configuration in a group is the next level "
"higher and finally an environment configuration in a group in a cloud is "
"the highest level of scoped precedence."
msgstr "Nota: the new environments object. The object graph of the morpheus blueprint structure gets merged and flattened at provision time based on the scope of the configurations provided as well as the users target cloud, group, and environment selection. In the Above example, a selective override was done for the `AWS Cali` cloud when using a Production Environment and deploying to the group `All Nubes Demo`. This specific example changes the plan to a larger size. Scoped configurations have various levels of precedence. Nube is the lowest level of precedence. a cloud configuration in a group is the next level higher and finally an environment configuration in a group in a cloud is the highest level of scoped precedence."

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:74
msgid "Getting Started"
msgstr "Getting Started"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:76
msgid ""
"To get started, it may be best to look at a simple App Blueprint "
"configuration. Docker templates are less complex than virtual machine "
"based templates so lets look at a Blueprint that deploys a single Nginx "
"container to a target cloud:"
msgstr "To get started, it puede/n be best a look en un/una simple App Blueprint configuration. Docker templates son less complex than virtual machine based templates so lets look en un/una Blueprint ese/esa deploys un/una single Nginx container a un/una target cloud:"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:78
msgid ""
"name: Simple Nginx\n"
"type: morpheus\n"
"tiers:\n"
"  Web:\n"
"    linkedTiers: []\n"
"    instances:\n"
"      - instance:\n"
"          type: docker\n"
"          name: Sample Nginx\n"
"        clouds:\n"
"          AWS Cali:\n"
"            instance:\n"
"              layout:\n"
"                code: docker-1.7-single\n"
"                id: 206\n"
"            volumes:\n"
"              - rootVolume: true\n"
"                name: root\n"
"                size: 1\n"
"            backup:\n"
"              createBackup: false\n"
"            config:\n"
"              dockerImageVersion: latest\n"
"              dockerRegistryId: ''\n"
"              dockerImage: nginx\n"
"            plan:\n"
"              id: 68\n"
"              code: container-128\n"
"            ports:\n"
"              - name: HTTP\n"
"                port: 80\n"
"                lb: HTTP"
msgstr "name: Simple Nginx\ntype: morpheus\ntiers:\n  Web:\n    linkedTiers: []\n    instances:\n      - instance:\n          type: docker\n          name: Sample Nginx\n        clouds:\n          AWS Cali:\n            instance:\n              layout:\n                code: docker-1.7-single\n                id: 206\n            volumes:\n              - rootVolume: true\n                name: root\n                size: 1\n            backup:\n              createBackup: false\n            config:\n              dockerImageVersion: latest\n              dockerRegistryId: ''\n              dockerImage: nginx\n            plan:\n              id: 68\n              code: container-128\n            ports:\n              - name: HTTP\n                port: 80\n                lb: HTTP"

#: ../../library/blueprints/appBlueprints/morpheus.rst:114
msgid ""
"Theres some useful things to look at in the above docker example. One is "
"there are different objects based on the different available "
"configuration options for the target provision type. These options are "
"actually data driven and can be extracted from the Inputs api in the "
"morpheus api doc. That is a useful resource to look at while building "
"morpheus blueprints or by using the `morpheus-cli` which provides prompts"
" for helping build custom morpheus app blueprints."
msgstr "Theres some useful things to look at in the above docker example. One is there are different objects based on the different available configuration options for the target provision type. These options are actually data driven and puede ser extracted from the Inputs api in the morpheus api doc. That is a useful resource to look at while building morpheus blueprints or by using the `morpheus-cli` which provides prompts for helping build custom morpheus app blueprints."

